  "sections": [
    {
      "key": "quickFacts",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<ul><li>Wild caught Betta albimarginata collected from Sebuku, from the Betta albimarginata complex.</li><li>Size: up to 5 cm (≈ 2″) in total length.</li><li>Temperature: 24–29 °C (75–85 °F).</li><li>pH 6.0–7.0; TDS 60–100 ppm.</li><li>IUCN Red List: Endangered.</li></ul>"
    },
    {
      "key": "beforeYouBuy",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>A great beginner fish for someone being introduced to the mouthbrooding betta world. This fish is slightly larger than the type collected in Malinau. Be sure to have the habitat ready before purchase.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Betta albimarginata from Sebuku is a rare mouthbrooding betta native to the forest streams of East Kalimantan, Borneo.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Prefers soft, clean, tannin-rich water with minimal current. Provide leaf litter, botanicals, and shaded areas to mimic their natural blackwater habitat.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "aquariumSize",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Recommend one pair per 5 gallons (about 19 litres). Best kept as pairs or small groups in spacious, heavily structured aquaria.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "waterConditions",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Soft, clean, tannin-rich water. pH 6.0–7.0; TDS 60–100 ppm. Though tolerant of higher TDS, they thrive best in softer water. Temperature: 24–29 °C (75–85 °F). Cooler water promotes longevity, while slightly warmer conditions encourage coloration and spawning behavior.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "equipment",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Provide stable, pristine water conditions and subdued lighting to minimize stress.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "diet",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Carnivorous. Currently feeding on live foods such as blackworms, and readily accepts frozen bloodworms and quality pellets once acclimated. Offer a varied, high-protein diet to maintain health and color.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "behaviour",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Somewhat territorial, but can coexist peacefully if visual barriers and hiding spots are available. Avoid aggressive or fast-moving species as tankmates.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "dimorphism",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>Males develop their most vibrant colors when in breeding condition, displaying striking contrast and graceful fin extension.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "bwaquatics",
      "sourceUrl": "https://bwaquatics.com/products/betta-albimarginata-sebuku",
      "html": "<p>They have a maroon or burgundy colored body with black and white seams on their fins; depending on locality, some may have a more orange body. Their best colors come out when ready to breed. Wild populations are classified as Endangered on the IUCN Red List due to rapid habitat destruction from palm oil plantation development.</p>"
    }
  ],
